---
layout: article
title: Inspect playback
description: Use the Appwrite Videos inspector to watch a playback session's experience score, buffer, bitrate, network requests, and manifests.
---

The **inspector** shows what the player in the Console is doing while it plays a video. Use it to check how fast a stream starts, whether it rebuffers, which renditions it picks, and what the player actually requested.

# Open the inspector {% #open-the-inspector %}

1. Open **Videos** in your project and select a video with at least one ready rendition.
2. Select **Inspector** under **Playback** in the video menu. It opens in its own window next to the video, so you can watch both at once.
3. Play the video. The inspector fills in as the session runs.

The inspector measures the session in your browser on your connection. To see how a stream behaves for slower viewers, throttle the network in your browser's developer tools and play again.

# Key metrics {% #key-metrics %}

If you are new to video, these few numbers tell you most of what matters:

| Metric | What it means | What good looks like |
|--------|---------------|----------------------|
| **Time to first frame** | How long from pressing play until the picture appears | As low as possible. Viewers notice anything near a second |
| **Rebuffering** | Time spent paused waiting for data after playback started | Zero, or close to it |
| **Average bitrate** | The quality the player sustained, on average | Close to your top rendition on a fast connection |
| **Quality switches** | How often the player changed rendition | A few early on, then stable |
| **Dropped frames** | Frames the device could not decode in time | Near zero. Many dropped frames point to a device or codec limit, not the network |

The **experience score** rolls these into one number for the session, and **Diagnostics** points out what pulled it down.

# Sections {% #sections %}

| Section | What it shows |
|---------|---------------|
| **Overview** | An **experience score** for the session, with diagnostics, time to first frame, rebuffering, average bitrate, quality switches, dropped frames, upscaling, bandwidth estimate, and watch time. Charts for bandwidth and bitrate, and buffer health |
| **Quality** | The bitrate ladder the player parsed, time spent on each rendition, and the history of quality switches |
| **Network** | Every segment request, with transferred bytes, average throughput, median time to first byte, and the slowest 5% load time |
| **Events** | Player events, newest first, such as loads, quality switches, stalls, and errors |
| **Manifests** | The master manifest and media playlists the player loaded, to copy, reload, or open in a new tab |
| **Device** | Codec support and playback capabilities of this device and browser, and whether each rendition can play smoothly here |

# Read the results {% #read-the-results %}

- **Slow start**: Check time to first frame, and the first segment requests in **Network**. A large first segment or a slow time to first byte delays the picture.
- **Rebuffering**: Check buffer health against the bandwidth estimate. If the player stays on a rendition above the available bandwidth, add a lower [profile](/docs/products/videos/profiles) to the ladder.
- **Soft picture**: Check **Upscaling** and the time spent per rendition. A ladder without a rendition near the player size forces upscaling.
- **A rendition never plays**: Check **Device**. The device may not support that rendition's codec.

# Test a ladder change {% #test-a-ladder-change %}

Use the inspector to check a change to your [profiles](/docs/products/videos/profiles) before you roll it out to every video:

1. Encode the new ladder on one test video.
2. In your browser's developer tools, throttle the network to a slow profile, such as a mobile connection.
3. Open the inspector and play the test video from the start. Note time to first frame, rebuffering, and which renditions the player used in **Quality**.
4. Repeat on an unthrottled connection, then compare with a video on the old ladder.

# Share a session {% #share-a-session %}

Use **Copy summary** to paste the session's key numbers into an issue or a chat, **Export session** to download the full session as a JSON file, and **Copy log** in **Events** to share the player events. This makes it easier to compare runs, or to show a teammate exactly what happened.
